# Target C-II — the full source hull has an explicit same-center `O(sqrt D)` disk sandwich

**Date:** 2026-09-17  
**Status:** proof-only finite corollary of the audited full-source Bonnesen geometry; no canonical promotion claimed  
**Scope:** every noncollinear global minimum-diameter source  
**Dependencies:** audited Section 58 full-source circumradius proof  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_full_source_same_center_disk_sandwich_2026_09_17.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN.`

The audited full-source geometry proves, for every noncollinear global minimizer with source hull `K`, diameter `D`, circumradius `R`, and inradius `r`,

\[
R-r<\eta_D,
\qquad
{D\over2}\le R<{D\over2}+\eta_D,
\qquad
\eta_D:=3^{1/4}\sqrt D.
\tag{SC.1}
\]

The proof did not record that the circumcenter and incenter themselves are automatically close. That observation upgrades (SC.1) to an explicit disk sandwich about one common center.

Let `o` be a circumcenter of `K` and `c` an incenter. Then

\[
\boxed{|c-o|\le R-r<\eta_D.}
\tag{SC.2}
\]

Consequently, for all sufficiently large `D`,

\[
\boxed{
B\!\left(o,{D\over2}-2\eta_D\right)
\subset K
\subset
B\!\left(o,{D\over2}+\eta_D\right).
}
\tag{SC.3}
\]

In particular

\[
\boxed{
d_H\!\left(K,B(o,D/2)\right)
<2\,3^{1/4}\sqrt D.
}
\tag{SC.4}
\]

Thus the entire source hull, not only the Type-II force hull and not only a subsequential normalized limit, is quantitatively near-circular about its own full-source circumcenter with an explicit `O(sqrt D)` Hausdorff rate.

This gives a source-wide normal/radial estimate. If `x in partial K` and `n` is any outward supporting unit normal at `x`, then

\[
n\cdot(x-o)
=h_K(n)
\ge {D\over2}-2\eta_D,
\qquad
|x-o|<{D\over2}+\eta_D.
\]

Hence

\[
\boxed{
\angle(n,x-o)
\le
\arccos\!\left(
{{D/2-2\eta_D}\over{D/2+\eta_D}}
\right)
=O(D^{-1/4}).
}
\tag{SC.5}
\]

This finite full-source statement can replace qualitative near-disk convergence whenever a later proof only needs hull support directions or radial localization.

---

## 1. The incenter lies close to every circumcenter

The inball satisfies

\[
B(c,r)\subset K
\subset B(o,R).
\tag{SC.6}
\]

If `c=o`, (SC.2) is immediate. Otherwise put

\[
e={c-o\over|c-o|}.
\]

The point

\[
z=c+re
\]

belongs to the closed inball and therefore to `K`. Its distance from `o` is

\[
|z-o|
=|c-o|+r.
\]

Because `K subset B(o,R)`,

\[
|c-o|+r\le R.
\]

Thus

\[
\boxed{|c-o|\le R-r.}
\tag{SC.7}
\]

Combining with the audited Bonnesen bound `R-r<eta_D` proves (SC.2).

---

## 2. Move the inball to the circumcenter

Let

\[
d:=|c-o|.
\]

Any point `z` satisfying

\[
|z-o|\le r-d
\]

also satisfies

\[
|z-c|
\le |z-o|+|o-c|
\le r,
\]

so

\[
B(o,r-d)\subset B(c,r)\subset K.
\tag{SC.8}
\]

From (SC.1) and (SC.2),

\[
r>R-\eta_D,
\qquad
d<\eta_D.
\]

Therefore

\[
r-d>R-2\eta_D
\ge {D\over2}-2\eta_D.
\tag{SC.9}
\]

For sufficiently large `D`, the right-hand radius is positive. Equations (SC.8)--(SC.9) prove the inner inclusion in (SC.3).

The outer inclusion follows immediately from

\[
K\subset B(o,R),
\qquad
R<{D\over2}+\eta_D.
\]

---

## 3. Hausdorff rate

Let

\[
R_-={D\over2}-2\eta_D,
\qquad
R_0={D\over2},
\qquad
R_+={D\over2}+\eta_D.
\]

Equation (SC.3) gives

\[
B(o,R_-)\subset K\subset B(o,R_+).
\]

For concentric convex bodies this implies

\[
d_H(K,B(o,R_0))
\le\max\{R_0-R_-,R_+-R_0\}
=2\eta_D,
\]

which proves (SC.4).

The estimate is finite and source-wide. No Type-I/Type-II split, KKT stress choice, contact-core deletion, or asymptotic subsequence is used beyond the already audited Section-58 inputs.

---

## 4. Support-normal consequence

Fix `x in partial K` and an outward supporting unit normal `n` there. Since the inner ball in (SC.3) is centered at `o`,

\[
h_K(n)=n\cdot(x-o)
\ge R_-.
\]

The outer inclusion gives

\[
|x-o|\le R_+.
\]

Therefore

\[
{n\cdot(x-o)\over|x-o|}
\ge {R_-\over R_+}.
\]

This proves (SC.5).

Since

\[
1-{R_-\over R_+}
=O(D^{-1/2}),
\]

the angle is `O(D^-1/4)` by the standard small-angle cosine expansion. This matches the scale of the audited diameter-star angular-width estimate, but now applies to every supporting normal of the full source hull.

---

## 5. Frontier use

The same-center sandwich does not itself contradict any Type-II survivor. Its value is that later source-level arguments no longer need to import qualitative force-hull near-disk convergence when they concern actual source-hull vertices, hull support normals, or fixed-slack almost-diameters.

In particular it is compatible with and strengthens the geometric setting of the shallow-tooth reflection branch: every loaded base vertex lying on the source hull has radial direction within `O(D^-1/4)` of its hull normal, uniformly and with an explicit full-source center.

**Disposition:** extracted an explicit finite `O(sqrt D)` same-center disk sandwich for the full source hull from the audited Bonnesen proof. C-II and Level I remain open.
